libpidesktop-ffmali — hardware video decode for the unmodified Firefox on Arm's Mali driver
===========================================================================================

Plaid Pi does not ship Firefox (Mozilla's trademark policy covers unaltered copies only); the first-click installer
installs the unmodified Firefox package from Ubuntu's Mozilla Team PPA. With Arm's proprietary Mali driver (libmali
g24p0) that Firefox cannot use the video frames the hardware decoder produces:
  8-bit   the NV12 chroma plane (DRM_FORMAT_GR88) does not import (EGL_BAD_MATCH) -> software decode
          (reported as Mozilla Bug 2078820)
  10-bit  the P010 luma plane (DRM_FORMAT_R16) imports as an integer texture      -> dark picture, colour only
This small library, loaded by Plaid Pi's Firefox launcher, works around both between Firefox and the driver: a
refused GR88/RG88 import is retried as R16 (same bytes per texel), and a plane the driver imported as an integer
texture is copied into an ordinary (normalised) texture when Firefox binds it. The source explains the details.
Not part of Firefox and not made or endorsed by Mozilla. If Firefox misbehaves, retry with PIDESKTOP_FFMALI=0 before
reporting to Mozilla, and mention this library in any report.

Measured 2026-10-12 on an Orange Pi 5B (libmali g24p0, Firefox 157.0 from the Mozilla Team PPA, unmodified):
ffmali-probe.py imports R16, GR88, RG88, GR1616 and R8 planes the way Firefox does and every sampled texel matches the
source bytes (with PIDESKTOP_FFMALI=0, R16 reads ~0 and GR88/RG88 are refused). In Firefox, 1080p 24 fps H.264
(8-bit), VP9 and AV1 (8- and 10-bit) clips decode on the VPU. A 4K luma plane's import and copy take ~2.2-2.5 ms per
frame in the probe. Not yet measured: Firefox's picture against a reference (checked by eye only), 4K and 10-bit 4K60
pacing in Firefox, HEVC.

Files
  libpidesktop-ffmali.c   the library (LGPL-2.1-or-later)
  build.sh                builds libpidesktop-ffmali.so.1 here (cross on an x86-64 build host, or --native on a board)
  ffmali-probe.py         board test: imports every plane format the way Firefox does and compares a grid of sampled
                          texels with the source bytes (PIDESKTOP_FFMALI=0 shows the unworked-around behaviour)
The image installs the library, and its complete source (libpidesktop-ffmali.c, build.sh, this README) in
/usr/share/pi-desktop/firefox/.

Install (customize-image.sh; pidesktop-install-app re-links the names when Firefox is installed)
  /usr/local/lib/pidesktop-firefox-mali/libpidesktop-ffmali.so.1
  libEGL.so, libGL.so -> libpidesktop-ffmali.so.1   (Firefox tries these names before the .so.1 ones)
  libGL.so.1 -> the Mali GLES library               (Firefox's GPU probe, as before 5.0; no libEGL.so.1 here)
The Firefox launcher (/usr/local/bin/firefox) puts that directory first on LD_LIBRARY_PATH. Programs Firefox starts
inherit it but load libEGL.so.1 / libGL.so.1, which are not this library, and the library only acts in processes whose
executable is under /usr/lib/firefox/ (PIDESKTOP_FFMALI=1 enables it anywhere, for tests).
The library assumes Firefox only samples the planes it fixes up; Plaid Pi's Firefox policy locks off the two settings
that would make Firefox render into them (media.ffmpeg.vaapi.force-surface-zero-copy, gfx.webrender.compositor).
Off switch: PIDESKTOP_FFMALI=0 (the launcher then also hides 10-bit from Firefox, the behaviour before 5.0).
Diagnostics: PIDESKTOP_FFMALI_LOG=1 logs every import and copy to Firefox's stderr.

If a future Firefox stops loading EGL/GL by these names, the library is not used: 8-bit falls back to software and
10-bit draws dark until PIDESKTOP_FFMALI=0 is set. The Firefox-side fix for 8-bit is the patch on Mozilla Bug 2078820.

Written with an LLM assistant (Anthropic's Claude), which drafted the library, build.sh and the probe and ran the tests
on the board; the maintainer directs and reviews the work and decides what ships.
